select 'bbbb0006-0000-4000-8000-000000000006', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Teachers often describe dance with five elements: body, action, space, time, and what?","options":["energy","harmony","perspective","temperature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The common framework is body, action, space, time, and energy - sometimes remembered by the initials BASTE."},{"prompt":"Which king helped turn ballet into a serious art at the French court and founded a royal academy of dance?","options":["Henry VIII","Louis XIV","Napoleon","Charlemagne"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Louis XIV danced leading roles himself and founded the Academie Royale de Danse in 1661 - see ''The birth of ballet''."},{"prompt":"Which composer wrote the music for Swan Lake, The Sleeping Beauty, and The Nutcracker?","options":["Ludwig van Beethoven","Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","Wolfgang Mozart","Johann Sebastian Bach"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tchaikovsky composed all three classical ballets, most choreographed by Marius Petipa."},{"prompt":"Isadora Duncan is remembered mainly as a pioneer of which kind of dance?","options":["classical ballet","modern dance","tap dance","ballroom tango"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Duncan rejected ballet''s rules and danced barefoot in free, natural movement - an early root of modern dance."},{"prompt":"The tango took shape in which part of the world?","options":["Vienna, Austria","the Rio de la Plata region of Argentina and Uruguay","Paris, France","Harlem, New York"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The tango grew in the port neighborhoods around Buenos Aires and Montevideo in the late 1800s."},{"prompt":"Hip-hop dance first emerged in the early 1970s in which place?","options":["London","the Bronx in New York City","Los Angeles","Chicago"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breaking grew up alongside hip-hop music and culture in the Bronx; related funk styles developed on the West Coast."},{"prompt":"Most ballet vocabulary (plie, tendu, arabesque) is taken from which language?","options":["Italian","French","Russian","Latin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ballet was codified at the French court, so its standard terms are French."},{"prompt":"Choreography is best described as the art of...","options":["choosing the music for a dance","composing and arranging movement","designing the costumes","selling the tickets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Choreography is the craft of composing movement into an ordered whole - the focus of the final lesson."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What dance is: body, action, space, time, energy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to define dance in your own words and break any piece of movement down into five working parts: body, action, space, time, and energy. These are the tools a choreographer uses and the lenses a viewer can look through. Warm-up, drawn from the diagnostic: the five elements are body, action, space, time, and one more - what is it? Hold your answer in mind. Dance is one of the oldest and most universal of human activities, found in every known culture, yet it leaves almost no physical trace, which is part of why learning to see it carefully matters so much. Think of this lesson as installing a vocabulary you will reuse in every lesson that follows, from ballet to hip-hop."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Dance is human movement chosen and shaped for its own sake - to express feeling, mark a ritual, tell a story, or simply give pleasure. That ordinary definition hides real variety, so analysts split dance into five elements. The body is the instrument: which parts move, and whether the whole figure or just a hand carries the phrase. Action is what the body does - steps, jumps, turns, gestures, stillness, and the transitions between them. Space is where movement happens: the level (high or low), the direction, the pathway traced on the floor, and the shape the body makes in the air. Time covers rhythm, tempo, duration, and how movement relates to music or to silence. Energy, sometimes called dynamics, is how a movement is done - sharp or smooth, heavy or light, sudden or sustained. The same step performed with different energy reads as a different idea. Together these five give you a complete checklist for describing anything you see."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"Every tradition in this course leans on the five elements in its own way, which is why they are worth learning first. Ballet prizes clear shapes in space and a light, controlled use of energy. Tap turns the body into a rhythm instrument, so time dominates. The tango organizes two bodies into shared space with sudden shifts of energy. Martha Graham built a whole modern technique around a single quality of energy - the contraction and release of the torso. Breaking explodes action and level, dropping the body to the floor and back up. No figure ''invented'' these elements; rather, choreographers from Marius Petipa to a street crew in the Bronx each emphasize some and quiet others. When you can name which elements a dance foregrounds, you can compare a court ballet and a social dance in the same breath, instead of treating them as unrelated worlds. That shared language is the point of starting here."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the five elements of dance. 2) A dancer freezes completely for four counts. Which element is most in play? 3) The same arm gesture is done once sharply and once smoothly. Which element changed? 4) A line of dancers traces a circle on the floor. Which element describes that pattern? 5) Clapping a steady beat with your feet mainly uses which element? Answers: 1) body, action, space, time, energy. 2) time, because stillness is a choice about duration (the body and action also matter, but the held count is the point). 3) energy, also called dynamics. 4) space, specifically the floor pathway. 5) time, since you are organizing rhythm. If you got most of these, you already have the core toolkit."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating ''action'' and ''energy'' as the same thing. Action is what step is done; energy is how it is done. A jump (action) can be floaty or forceful (energy). Mistake: forgetting that stillness is part of dance - a held pose is a deliberate use of time and space, not an absence of dance. Mistake: assuming music is required; many dances work in silence or to breath and footfall alone. Exit ticket: (a) Which element covers tempo and rhythm? (b) Which element covers whether a movement is sharp or smooth? Answers: time; energy. Carry these five words forward - the rest of the course is really one long exercise in using them on real dances."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep two words apart: ''movement'' is any physical motion, while ''dance'' is movement shaped on purpose for meaning, ritual, or delight.', 12),
  (3, 'Dance in early and traditional cultures', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson lets you place dance in human history and tell the difference between ritual dance and folk dance by what each one is for. Warm-up: before reading, guess what role dance might have played before writing existed - what could a community do with shared movement that words alone could not? Dance almost certainly predates recorded history. Scholars point to a famous set of rock paintings at the Bhimbetka shelters in India and to painted tomb scenes in ancient Egypt as some of the earliest surviving evidence of dancing, though the dances themselves are long gone. Because movement vanishes the instant it is made, we study early dance through pictures, carvings, writing about it, and living traditions passed down by practice. Keep that limitation in mind: we know dance was everywhere, but we rarely know exactly how it looked."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Across early and traditional societies, dance tends to serve the group rather than the stage. Ritual dance is bound to belief and ceremony: it marks birth, initiation, marriage, death, harvest, war, and worship, and often aims to bring about an outcome - rain, healing, a good hunt, the favor of the ancestors. Folk dance is the everyday social dance of a community, learned by growing up inside it and performed at festivals and celebrations for belonging and joy rather than for a paying audience. The line between them blurs, since a harvest dance can be both sacred and festive. What unites traditional dance is that it is participatory and communal: people dance together, usually in lines, circles, or couples, to shared music and known steps, and the knowledge lives in bodies and memory rather than in notation. This is dance as something you do, not only something you watch."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"Traditional dance spans the globe. In West Africa, polyrhythmic drumming drives dances tied to community and ceremony, with movement answering specific drum patterns. In India, temple traditions grew into highly codified classical forms such as Bharatanatyam, where precise hand gestures (mudras) carry meaning. Many cultures favor the circle or line: the Greek syrtos and kalamatianos join hands in open chains, Jewish celebration includes the circling hora, and ring and line dances recur across the Balkans and eastern Europe. Court and folk sometimes meet, as in the stately, patterned dances of imperial China and Japan. European folk dance ranges from the Irish set and step traditions to Morris dancing in England. No single person authored these forms; they are collective inheritances, revised slowly over generations. When you watch one, ask what it is for - celebration, worship, courtship, storytelling - because function shapes the movement far more than any individual choreographer does."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In one sentence, how does ritual dance differ from folk dance by purpose? 2) Why do we know so little about exactly how prehistoric dance looked? 3) Name two common group formations in traditional dance. 4) In Indian classical dance, what are mudras? 5) True or false: folk dance is mainly made to be watched by a ticket-buying audience. Answers: 1) ritual dance serves belief and ceremony and often aims at an outcome, while folk dance is communal social dance for celebration and belonging. 2) movement leaves no direct trace, so we rely on images, writing, and living tradition. 3) the circle and the line (also couples). 4) codified hand gestures that carry specific meanings. 5) false - folk dance is participatory, danced by the community itself."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining a single worldwide ''original'' dance; traditions arose separately and look very different. Mistake: ranking traditional dances as simpler or lesser than theatrical ones - many, such as Bharatanatyam, are extremely refined and demanding. Mistake: assuming ritual and folk never overlap; a single festival dance can be sacred and social at once. Exit ticket: (a) Name one early source scholars use to study ancient dance. (b) Is traditional dance mostly performed for an audience or danced by the community itself? Answers: rock paintings such as those at Bhimbetka, or Egyptian tomb scenes; it is mostly participatory, danced by the community. You now have the backdrop against which staged forms like ballet will stand out."}]}'::jsonb, 'Avoid calling older or non-Western dances ''primitive''; the accurate words are ''traditional'', ''folk'', or ''ritual'', which describe function rather than ranking.', 13),
  (4, 'The birth of ballet and the classical age', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Here you will trace how ballet grew from a court entertainment into a codified theatrical art, and you will be able to name the figures behind the classical ballets still performed today. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: which French king helped establish ballet and founded a royal dance academy? Hold that name. Ballet is important out of proportion to its age because it was the first Western dance to be written about, taught systematically, and given a fixed technical vocabulary. That codification is why a dancer in Tokyo and a dancer in Toronto can rehearse the same steps under the same French names. Understanding ballet''s history also sets up everything that reacts against it later, from the Romantic era to modern dance."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Ballet began in the lavish court spectacles of the Italian Renaissance and was carried to France, where it became a royal passion. In the 1600s, King Louis XIV - himself a keen dancer who appeared as the sun in a court ballet - founded the Academie Royale de Danse in 1661, giving dance official standing and pushing toward a standard technique. Over the next century ballet moved off the ballroom floor and onto the raised stage, dancers became trained professionals rather than courtiers, and the five positions of the feet and much of today''s vocabulary were fixed. The art then migrated east. By the late 1800s its center of gravity had shifted to Imperial Russia, where state support and great teaching produced the grand, full-evening story ballets we now call ''classical''. This is the arc to remember: Italian courts, French codification, Russian flowering."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"The classical age belongs above all to the choreographer Marius Petipa, a Frenchman who worked for decades at the Imperial Theatres in St. Petersburg. Working with the composer Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky, Petipa shaped the repertoire that defines ballet for most audiences: The Sleeping Beauty in 1890 and The Nutcracker in 1892 (the latter choreographed with his assistant Lev Ivanov), while the enduring 1895 staging of Swan Lake was created by Petipa and Ivanov together. These works set the template of the grand classical ballet - a story in several acts, alternating dramatic mime with pure dance set-pieces, a corps de ballet moving in disciplined unison, and a virtuoso pas de deux for the leads. The dancing is organized around symmetry, clean line, turnout, and pointe work for the ballerina. When you watch a classical ballet, you are watching a nineteenth-century Russian structure performed in a seventeenth-century French language."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In which country''s courts did ballet first take shape? 2) What did Louis XIV found in 1661, and why did it matter? 3) Name the choreographer most associated with classical story ballets. 4) Which composer wrote Swan Lake, The Sleeping Beauty, and The Nutcracker? 5) What is the corps de ballet? Answers: 1) the Italian Renaissance courts (ballet then flourished in France). 2) the Academie Royale de Danse, which gave dance official status and pushed toward a standard technique. 3) Marius Petipa. 4) Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky. 5) the large group of ensemble dancers who move together, often in unison, behind the soloists. These names return in every later lesson on ballet."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking ballet started in Russia; Russia perfected the classical story ballet, but the form was born in Italian and French courts. Mistake: crediting Tchaikovsky with the choreography - he wrote the music, while Petipa and Ivanov made the steps. Mistake: assuming women always danced on pointe from the start; pointe work developed gradually and became central only in the 1800s. Exit ticket: (a) Which king gave ballet royal backing and founded a dance academy? (b) Name the Petipa and Tchaikovsky ballet first staged in 1890. Answers: Louis XIV; The Sleeping Beauty. You are now ready to see how the Romantic era and then modern dance pushed against this classical order."}]}'::jsonb, 'A ''ballet'' is a whole staged work; a ''ballerina'' is a female ballet dancer; the technique and the art form are simply ''ballet'' - do not write ''a ballet dancer does ballets'' when you mean the technique.', 14),
  (5, 'Romantic ballet and the modern dance revolution', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson covers two turning points: the Romantic ballet of the early 1800s and the modern dance rebellion of the early 1900s. You will be able to say what each one wanted and name its central figures. Warm-up: the diagnostic asked which pioneer danced barefoot in free, natural movement against ballet''s rules - recall that name. These two moments bracket a shift in taste. Romantic ballet made the ballerina a weightless, otherworldly ideal; a century later, modern dancers rejected exactly that ideal and put weight, breath, and raw feeling back into the body. Seeing them side by side shows how an art form changes by both refining and revolting against what came before."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Romantic ballet flowered in Paris around the 1830s and 1840s, part of the wider Romantic movement''s love of the supernatural, the exotic, and doomed longing. On stage this meant ghostly women - sylphs, spirits, enchanted brides - danced in long white skirts under gaslight, with the ballerina rising onto pointe to appear to float free of gravity. Nearly a century later, a very different impulse arose in the United States and Germany. The modern dancers argued that ballet had become artificial and rule-bound, so they discarded the pointe shoe, the turnout, and the fairy-tale plots. They danced barefoot, used the pull of gravity and the weight of the body, and built new personal techniques to express inner states and real human struggle. Romantic ballet idealized the body into a spirit; modern dance reclaimed the body as a living, breathing, grounded instrument."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"The signature Romantic ballets are La Sylphide, created in Paris in 1832 with the dancer Marie Taglioni as the elusive forest spirit, and Giselle of 1841, with music by Adolphe Adam, in which a betrayed girl becomes one of the vengeful ghostly Wilis. Both remain in repertory and still define the white, moonlit, ethereal style. Modern dance has its own founders. Isadora Duncan, around 1900, danced barefoot in loose tunics inspired by ancient Greece, insisting movement should flow naturally from the solar plexus. A generation later Martha Graham built the most influential modern technique of all, based on the ''contraction and release'' of the torso with the breath; her 1944 work Appalachian Spring, set to a commissioned score by Aaron Copland, is a landmark. Figures like Ruth St. Denis and Doris Humphrey round out the founding generation."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In which city and rough decade did Romantic ballet flower? 2) Name one famous Romantic ballet and say what kind of figure the heroine becomes or is. 3) What technical device helped make the Romantic ballerina appear weightless? 4) Who pioneered barefoot, free movement around 1900? 5) What is the name of Martha Graham''s core technique? Answers: 1) Paris, in the 1830s to 1840s. 2) La Sylphide (a forest spirit) or Giselle (who becomes one of the ghostly Wilis). 3) dancing on pointe, under soft gaslight. 4) Isadora Duncan. 5) contraction and release, built around the breath and the torso. These contrasts will sharpen your eye for style."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: lumping all old ballet together - Romantic ballet (ghostly, white, 1830s) came before the grander classical ballets of Petipa later in the century. Mistake: thinking modern dance is just ''ballet without shoes''; it is a set of new techniques with their own principles, like Graham''s contraction and release. Mistake: crediting a single inventor of modern dance - Duncan, St. Denis, Graham, and Humphrey all contributed. Exit ticket: (a) Name the 1841 Romantic ballet with music by Adolphe Adam. (b) Which choreographer created Appalachian Spring? Answers: Giselle; Martha Graham. Next you will see how ballet itself was reinvented in the twentieth century."}]}'::jsonb, 'Capitalize ''Romantic'' when it names the artistic movement of the early 1800s; lowercase ''romantic'' only when you mean ''to do with love''.', 14),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The five commonly taught elements of dance are body, action, space, time, and...","options":["melody","energy","gravity","color"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Energy, also called dynamics, covers how a movement is done - sharp or smooth, heavy or light."},{"prompt":"Ritual dance is best distinguished from folk dance by...","options":["its faster tempo","its ceremonial purpose and aim at an outcome","its use of electric instruments","being danced only by professionals"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ritual dance is tied to belief and ceremony; folk dance is everyday communal celebration."},{"prompt":"Classical story ballets like The Sleeping Beauty were choreographed mainly by...","options":["Tchaikovsky","Marius Petipa","Isadora Duncan","Louis XIV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Petipa made the choreography; Tchaikovsky composed the music."},{"prompt":"Romantic ballet of the 1830s is best described as...","options":["grounded and barefoot","ethereal, with ghostly women dancing on pointe","a style of street dance","a type of ballroom couple dance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Romantic ballet favored sylphs and spirits, pointe work, and a weightless, moonlit look - as in La Sylphide and Giselle."},{"prompt":"Martha Graham''s core modern-dance technique is built around...","options":["turnout and pointe work","contraction and release of the torso","polyrhythmic footwork","partner lifts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Graham built movement from the contraction and release of the torso, driven by the breath."},{"prompt":"Ballet''s standard technical vocabulary is in which language, reflecting where it was codified?","options":["Italian","French","Russian","Greek"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ballet was codified at the French court under Louis XIV, so its terms are French."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Twentieth-century ballet and Balanchine', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson explains how ballet broke out of the nineteenth-century story format and became a modern art, and it introduces the choreographer who did more than anyone to make that happen. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: which company is George Balanchine most associated with? Hold your answer. The twentieth century gave ballet two big jolts: first a daring, scandalous company that toured Europe, then a stripped-down, music-driven style that took root in America. By the end you should be able to describe what ''neoclassical'' ballet is and why a ballet can be gripping without telling a story at all. This matters for appreciation, because much of the ballet made in the last hundred years has no plot to follow."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Two forces reshaped twentieth-century ballet. The first was the Ballets Russes, a touring company led by the impresario Sergei Diaghilev in the early 1900s, which treated ballet as a laboratory, pairing bold choreographers with leading modern composers and painters. Its premiere of The Rite of Spring in 1913, with music by Igor Stravinsky, was so jagged and primal that it reportedly provoked an uproar in the theatre. The second force was neoclassicism, which kept the clarity and rigor of classical technique but threw out the elaborate plots, mime, and scenery. A neoclassical ballet is often plotless: its subject is the dancing itself and its relationship to the music. This is ballet turned toward pure form - speed, line, rhythm, and structure - rather than fairy tales, a direct parallel to abstract painting in the same decades."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"The towering figure is George Balanchine, trained in Russia, launched through Diaghilev''s Ballets Russes, and later settled in the United States, where he co-founded the New York City Ballet in 1948. Balanchine is the father of neoclassical and plotless ballet. His Apollo of 1928, to Stravinsky, is often called the first neoclassical ballet; his Serenade of 1934, to Tchaikovsky, was the first work he made in America; and the spare, modern Agon of 1957, again with Stravinsky, pushed the style to its edge. Balanchine''s partnership with Stravinsky is one of the great composer-choreographer collaborations, echoing Petipa and Tchaikovsky a century before. His ballets are fast, musical, and clean, treating the body as a visual instrument for the score. Through his company and school he shaped generations of American dancers and defined what much of modern ballet looks like today."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Who led the Ballets Russes? 2) Which 1913 Stravinsky ballet reportedly caused an uproar at its premiere? 3) What does ''neoclassical'' ballet keep, and what does it drop? 4) Which company did Balanchine co-found, and in what year? 5) Name one Balanchine ballet set to music by Stravinsky. Answers: 1) Sergei Diaghilev. 2) The Rite of Spring. 3) it keeps classical technique and line but drops the story, mime, and heavy scenery. 4) the New York City Ballet, in 1948. 5) Apollo (1928) or Agon (1957); Serenade uses Tchaikovsky. If plotless ballet felt strange before, you now have a way to watch it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming every ballet tells a story - much twentieth-century ballet is deliberately plotless, and the dancing itself is the subject. Mistake: confusing ''classical'' (Petipa''s story ballets) with ''neoclassical'' (Balanchine''s stripped-down, music-first works). Mistake: thinking Balanchine composed his scores; like Petipa, he choreographed to music by others, above all Stravinsky. Exit ticket: (a) What is the subject of a plotless neoclassical ballet, if not a story? (b) Who is considered the father of neoclassical ballet? Answers: the dancing itself and its relationship to the music; George Balanchine. Next we leave the theatre for the ballroom and the dance floor."}]}'::jsonb, '''Neoclassical'' (one word, no hyphen) describes ballet that keeps classical technique but strips away story and heavy scenery; do not confuse it with ''classical'', which usually means the Petipa story ballets.', 13),
  (8, 'Social and popular dance: waltz, tango, swing, and Latin', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson covers the dances people do for themselves - at parties, in ballrooms, and on dance floors - and you will be able to place the waltz, the tango, swing, and the major Latin dances by where they came from. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: in which region did the tango take shape? Social dance differs from the stage forms we have studied: it is danced for the pleasure of the dancers rather than for an audience, usually in couples, and it spreads by popularity rather than by a single choreographer. These forms also travelled far from their origins, often crossing class and national lines, so their history is a map of migration and mixing as much as of steps."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Social dance tends to begin somewhere specific and local, then sweep outward once it catches on. The waltz did exactly this: a close-hold turning couple dance that rose from central European folk roots and became the sensation of nineteenth-century Vienna and beyond, scandalous at first for how closely the partners held each other. The tango grew in the working-class port neighborhoods of Buenos Aires and Montevideo, around the Rio de la Plata, late in the 1800s, then conquered the ballrooms of Paris and the world in the 1910s. Swing dancing rose with jazz in the United States in the 1920s and 1930s. And a cluster of Latin dances carried African and European influences out of the Caribbean and Latin America into global popularity. The common thread is a social origin, a couple form, and a journey from local floor to international fashion."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"The waltz is danced in triple time (a strong one-two-three) and is forever linked with Vienna and the lilting waltzes of Johann Strauss II. The tango is marked by a close, dramatic embrace, sharp pauses, and intricate footwork, and its music centers on the sighing bandoneon. Swing dance, above all the energetic Lindy Hop, was forged by Black dancers in Harlem ballrooms such as the Savoy and set to big-band jazz, full of bounce, breakaways, and aerial moves. The Latin family is broad: the Cuban son gave rise to salsa; the Brazilian samba drives Carnival; and the cha-cha-cha, rumba, and mambo round out the ballroom Latin group, all rich in hip motion and syncopated rhythm. Unlike ballet, these forms rarely have a single named author; they are collective creations shaped on crowded floors and then codified later for teaching and competition."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what musical time is the waltz danced, and which city is it tied to? 2) Where did the tango originate? 3) Name the famous Harlem swing dance and the ballroom associated with it. 4) Which Cuban style is a main root of salsa? 5) What tends to be true of who ''invented'' a social dance? Answers: 1) triple time (one-two-three), tied to Vienna. 2) the Rio de la Plata region, around Buenos Aires and Montevideo. 3) the Lindy Hop, linked with the Savoy Ballroom. 4) the son. 5) there is usually no single inventor; social dances are collective creations that spread by popularity. Now you can read a dance floor historically."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming these dances were always respectable; both the waltz and the tango were once considered shocking for their closeness. Mistake: treating ''Latin dance'' as one thing - salsa, samba, rumba, cha-cha-cha, and mambo have distinct roots and rhythms. Mistake: looking for a single choreographer; social dances grow on the floor and are only codified afterward for teaching and contests. Exit ticket: (a) Which dance is set to the music of Johann Strauss II and danced in triple time? (b) With which music style did swing dancing rise? Answers: the waltz; jazz (big-band jazz). Next we return to the theatre and the stage, where jazz, tap, and Broadway took these popular energies professional."}]}'::jsonb, '''Ballroom'' usually names the partnered competitive and social forms (waltz, foxtrot, tango); ''social dance'' is the broader term for any dance ordinary people do together for fun. Ballroom is a kind of social dance.', 13),
  (9, 'Jazz, tap, Broadway, and the rise of street dance', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson follows a distinctly American line of dance - tap, jazz, and the Broadway musical - and then the emergence of hip-hop and street dance, and you will be able to name key figures in each. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: in which part of New York did hip-hop dance first emerge in the 1970s? These forms share a common root in African American culture and a common spirit of rhythmic invention and personal style. They also moved between the street, the social floor, and the professional stage, blurring the line between popular and theatrical dance. By the end you should be able to connect a tap routine, a Broadway number, and a b-boy''s floor spin as parts of one broad, improvisation-loving tradition."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Tap dance turns the feet into a percussion instrument, fusing African rhythmic sensibility with Irish and British step-dancing on American soil; the sound is as important as the shape. Jazz dance is a theatrical style, loose, syncopated, and grounded, that grew alongside jazz music and absorbed steps from social and vernacular dance. The Broadway musical then gave these styles a home: over the twentieth century, dance in American musical theatre grew from decorative numbers into storytelling in its own right, with choreographers treated as authors. Decades later, a wholly new movement culture arose in New York and California. Hip-hop and the related West Coast funk styles came out of young people''s social dancing - at Bronx block parties and in California streets - and built a competitive, improvisational world of crews, cyphers, and battles. The connecting idea across all of these is rhythm, individuality, and improvisation."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"In tap, Bill ''Bojangles'' Robinson brought elegant clarity, the Nicholas Brothers dazzled with acrobatic flair on film, and in our own era Savion Glover renewed the form with dense, funk-driven rhythm. Jazz and Broadway produced great dance-makers: Katherine Dunham wove African and Caribbean movement into concert and theatrical dance; Agnes de Mille''s dream ballet in Oklahoma! (1943) showed dance could carry a plot; Jerome Robbins choreographed the electric street movement of West Side Story (1957); and Bob Fosse created an unmistakable style of angled hips, turned-in knees, and precise isolations. In street dance, breaking (b-boying and b-girling) developed in the Bronx, while popping and locking - the latter credited to Don Campbell - grew from the California funk scene. These styles are danced in a cypher, a circle where dancers take turns, and tested in battles."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which two broad traditions fused to create American tap? 2) What did Agnes de Mille''s work in Oklahoma! help prove about dance in musicals? 3) Name the choreographer known for isolations, angled hips, and turned-in knees. 4) What is the original name for ''breakdancing''? 5) What is a cypher? Answers: 1) African rhythmic traditions and Irish/British step-dancing. 2) that dance could advance the story, not just decorate it. 3) Bob Fosse. 4) breaking (b-boying/b-girling). 5) the circle in which street dancers take turns performing. If these came easily, you can trace one rhythmic tradition from tap shoes to the Bronx floor."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling the Bronx dance ''breakdancing'' as if that were its real name; practitioners call it breaking, and ''breakdancing'' was a later media label. Mistake: treating Broadway dance as mere decoration; since works like Oklahoma! and West Side Story, it often drives the story. Mistake: lumping all street dance together - breaking, popping, and locking have different origins and movement logic. Exit ticket: (a) Which choreographer staged the dances in West Side Story? (b) Who is credited with originating locking? Answers: Jerome Robbins; Don Campbell. One lesson remains: pulling all of this together into the skill of watching a dance well."}]}'::jsonb, '''Tap'' names the percussive shoe dance; ''jazz dance'' is a theatrical style; ''breaking'' is the original name for the dance that the news media later labeled ''breakdancing''. Prefer ''breaking'' when you can.', 13),
  (10, 'How to watch a dance: choreography and composition', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This final lesson turns everything into a practical skill: watching a dance work with an informed, attentive eye. By the end you will have a repeatable method for describing and judging what you see, in any style from this course. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: in one phrase, what is choreography? Hold your answer. Appreciation is not about having opinions ready-made; it is about noticing. A trained viewer sees structure, motif, and dynamics where an untrained viewer sees only ''dancing''. The good news is that the tools are the same five elements you learned in lesson two, now joined by a few ideas about how movement is composed over time. This is the payoff of the whole course."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Choreography is the art of composing and arranging movement into an ordered whole, and composition is how a choreographer builds that whole out of parts. Start small and zoom out. A step is one movement; a phrase is a short sequence, the dance equivalent of a musical phrase; and the structure is how phrases are grouped, repeated, and varied across the work. Watch for a motif - a signature movement idea that returns and develops. Watch how many bodies do the same thing at once (unison) or in overlapping succession (canon), and how the dancers are arranged and move through space (the spatial design). Attend to dynamics, the sharp-or-smooth energy of the movement, and to the relationship with the music: is the dance riding the beat, ignoring it, or arguing with it? Finally ask what holds it together - a story, a theme, a mood, or pure formal pattern."},{"heading":"Key forms & figures","text":"Each tradition rewards a slightly different focus, so aim your attention accordingly. In a Petipa classic, track the architecture: the corps de ballet in symmetrical unison, then the structured climax of the grand pas de deux. In a Balanchine ballet, drop the search for plot and instead watch how movement answers the music, phrase by phrase. In Graham''s modern work, feel the dynamics - the pull of contraction and release and the weight of the body. On a social floor, watch the lead-and-follow conversation between partners in a tango or the breakaways of the Lindy Hop. In a cypher, notice how a b-boy sets a rhythmic idea and then varies it under pressure. There is no single correct response, but there are richer and poorer ones: the richer response names what it sees - the element, the motif, the structure - before it reaches for a verdict."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Put these in order from smallest to largest: structure, step, phrase. 2) What is a motif in choreography? 3) Many dancers doing the same movement at the same time is called what? 4) The same movement passed from dancer to dancer in overlapping succession is called what? 5) When watching a plotless Balanchine ballet, what should you track instead of a story? Answers: 1) step, phrase, structure. 2) a signature movement idea that recurs and develops through the work. 3) unison. 4) canon. 5) the relationship between the movement and the music - how the dancing answers the score. If these come easily, you can now describe any dance before you judge it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: judging a dance before describing it - ''I liked it'' or ''it was boring'' says nothing until you name what you saw. Mistake: demanding a story from every work, when many pieces are built on theme, mood, or pure pattern. Mistake: watching only the lead dancer and missing the composition - the spacing, the corps, the canon, the recurring motif. Exit ticket: (a) Define choreography in one phrase. (b) Name two compositional tools a choreographer uses to organize many bodies in time. Answers: the art of composing and arranging movement into an ordered whole; unison and canon (also motif, repetition, and spatial design). You now have both the history and the eye to watch dance with real understanding - the goal of this course."}]}'::jsonb, 'A ''step'' is a single movement; a ''phrase'' is a short sequence of movements; the ''composition'' or ''structure'' is how those phrases are arranged into the whole work - zoom between these three levels as you watch.', 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0006-0000-4000-8000-000000000006' and l.position = v.pos
);
